HTTP Parameter Pollution (OHPP) is a technique where an attacker crafts a small input to redirect a browser to an unintended target page. The name comes from how the attacker uses multiple `±` characters (items) to move the target toward the unwanted direction. Think of it as a jigsaw puzzle where a cutter set flips the pieces, hoping you didn't notice the order initially. For WordPress sites, that means routes without the fifth item (footer index) or updating `/wp-admin/settings-wp.php` and `/wp-content/uploads/` to include the suffix.

Here's how to catch unwanted redirects: add filters like `add_filter` or set `REWRITE_HTTP_REQUEST_METHOD’. Most platforms block these unless you've got proper 403 policies and ROBOTS.TXT robots.txt blocking. Example:

if (!('$uri' == 'https://example.com/login')) {
rewrite_rule('^/login?$', 'https://example.com/', 301);
rewrite_rule('^/wp-admin/admin.php$', 'https://example.com/wp-admin/admin.php');
rewrite_rule('post_path_tag_fix(\'actor.php?auth_login=false\'), \'https://example.com/login\');
rewrite_rule('post_path_tag_fix(\'post_content.php?module_id=3\', \'http:\\example\.org\\post_media\; buried\');'}
